> Serial work and the cost of certification

A complete round performs n−1 comparisons for n≥1. Reverse order has n(n−1)/2
inversions and therefore exactly that many exchanges. Sorted input still
executes one full odd/even round, so its work is linear rather than constant.
The driver uses O(n²) worst-case serial comparisons; disjoint comparisons offer
a possible parallel schedule, not an implemented timing result.

The controlled families below come from the reusable native adjacent-sort
fixture. Values labelled “phases” include the final certification round; they
are measured operation counts, not CPU time or a claimed speedup over another
sort.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/odd_even_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/odd_even_sort.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gnome_sort.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, ascending, descending;
    std::print("| n | ascending comparisons | reverse comparisons | reverse swaps | actual reverse phases " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}) {
        auto f = dm::algorithms::adjacent_sort_inputs(n);
        auto a =
            dm::algorithms::odd_even_sort_run(f.ascending), d =
            dm::algorithms::odd_even_sort_run(f.descending);
        x.push_back(n);
        ascending.push_back(a.work.comparisons);
        descending.push_back(d.work.comparisons);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.work.comparisons,
                   d.work.comparisons, d.work.swaps, d.work.phases);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(x, ascending, "already sorted", dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}).points(x, ascending, dm::graphics::RGBA{37, 99, 235, 255}, 3.).lines(x, descending, dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, descending, dm::graphics::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 3);
    p.legend({"already sorted", "reverse input"}, {{37, 99, 235}, {220, 38, 38}}).title("Odd-even sort: actual serial comparisons").x_label("input integers").y_label("actual key comparisons").save_svg("build/odd_even_work.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | ascending comparisons | reverse comparisons | reverse swaps | actual reverse phases |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2 |
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2 |
| 2 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 4 |
| 4 | 3 | 9 | 6 | 6 |
| 8 | 7 | 35 | 28 | 10 |
| 16 | 15 | 135 | 120 | 18 |
| 32 | 31 | 527 | 496 | 34 |
\image(figures/odd_even_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual key comparisons and reverse-input exchanges on native controlled input families.)
\alttext(Sorted input needs one complete certification round; reverse input requires quadratic serial work.)

> Empty input, stability and owned history

The original driver visits both phases once even for an empty vector, without
comparing a key. Keeping that behavior makes the distinction between phase
visits and comparisons explicit. Duplicate-key identities are carried by the
actual exchanges; their final order is the stability certificate shown below.

Ordinary sorting owns the by-value input/output buffer and otherwise uses
constant working state. The richer run adds n original identities. A trace
additionally owns its input, every actual pair record and full values/IDs after
every phase. Its exact logical quota is3n+C+P(1+2n), for C actual comparisons
and P actual phases. Full snapshots can therefore retain O(n³) scalar entries,
even though sorting itself takes O(n²) work. The default cap is1000000 logical
units, zero disables it, and admission precedes each retained copy/append. A
failure returns no partial trace and never mutates an lvalue caller’s array.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/odd_even_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/odd_even_sort.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> inputs{{}, {7}, {2, 1, 2, 1}, {4, 3, 2, 1},
                                         {std::numeric_limits<int>::max(),
                                          std::numeric_limits<int>::min()}};
    std::vector<std::string> labels{"empty", "singleton", "duplicates",
                                    "reverse four", "signed limits"};
    std::vector<double> phases;
    std::print("| control | actual sorted values | actual original IDs | comparisons | phases | capture " "units |\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < inputs.size(); ++i) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::odd_even_sort_trace(inputs[i]);
        phases.push_back(t.run.work.phases);
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | `{}` | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i],
                   t.run.output, t.run.original_indices, t.run.work.comparisons,
                   t.run.work.phases, t.captured_entries);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(phases, labels, "actual phases");
    p.title("Odd-even controls: phase visits include certification").x_label(
        "explicit native input").y_label("actual phase visits").save_svg(
        "build/odd_even_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| control | actual sorted values | actual original IDs | comparisons | phases | capture units |
| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| empty | `[]` | `[]` | 0 | 2 | 2 |
| singleton | `[7]` | `[0]` | 0 | 2 | 9 |
| duplicates | `[1, 1, 2, 2]` | `[1, 3, 0, 2]` | 9 | 6 | 75 |
| reverse four | `[1, 2, 3, 4]` | `[3, 2, 1, 0]` | 9 | 6 | 75 |
| signed limits | `[-2147483648, 2147483647]` | `[1, 0]` | 2 | 4 | 28 |
\image(figures/odd_even_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual empty, singleton, duplicate, reverse and extreme-integer controls.)
\alttext(Duplicate original IDs remain increasing within equal keys; a reverse four-element sort executes more than four total phases because certification is included.)

